1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,160
 This video is all about corrosion, the natural breakdown of engineering materials.

2
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:08,960
 This video will be particularly focused on metals, which corrode in an electrochemical reaction.

3
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:12,080
 Other materials don't really corrode the same way, if at all.

4
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:16,160
 Ceramics aren't particularly susceptible to corrosion, nor plastics and polymers,

5
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:19,680
 so you can break these down by other means for you to be light and fire.

6
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:24,320
 We'll talk a little bit more about corrosion of those specific materials when we meet them in the coming weeks.

7
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:29,120
 Corrosion is generally viewed in a pretty negative life by engineers and designers of components.

8
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:34,160
 It has a tendency to break down and pit and rust and destroy our beautiful designs.

9
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:38,560
 But on top of being aesthetically displeasing, corrosion also generally has the

10
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:42,640
 dangerous tendency to worsen the material properties of our components,

11
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:46,640
 weaken our structures. And as a result, understanding why corrosion forms,

12
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:50,160
 how to prevent it and how to limit is important for you as engineers,

13
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:53,360
 especially if you're going to be designing safety critical components.

14
00:00:53,360 --> 00:00:56,400
 Corrosion is generally the result of an electrochemical reaction

15
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:59,760
 known as an oxidation reduction or redox reaction.

16
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:02,560
 Now, I'm getting pretty good at saying this, but I'll say it again.

17
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:05,120
 This isn't a chemistry course, and I'm not going to get into the weeds

18
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:06,880
 of how a redox reaction works.

19
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:08,960
 But if you're interested, what's wrong with you?

20
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:11,840
 I've put some resources down in the video description that you can check out.

21
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:14,240
 The abridged version of those resources is as follows.

22
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:18,320
 A redox reaction occurs between two participants, an anode and a cathode.

23
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:20,800
 In the kind of redox reaction that causes corrosion,

24
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:24,560
 the anodic participant gives up an electron to the cathodic participant.

25
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:28,240
 The anode then becomes positively charged, it's said to be oxidized,

26
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:31,840
 and the cathode becomes negatively charged. It's said to be reduced.

27
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:36,480
 This process, this ionization of the anode forms an oxide on the anode.

28
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:40,080
 In the case of some materials, this oxidation is actually rather harmless.

29
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:44,240
 It forms the thin film over the anode, sealing it from the cathode and preventing any further

30
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:47,520
 corrosion from taking place. In lots of other materials, however,

31
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:51,680
 including perhaps the most famous one, iron oxide rust, the oxide layer forms

32
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:56,320
 as a weak flaky poo on top of the material that's easily removed,

33
00:01:56,320 --> 00:01:59,920
 exposing fresh anodic material to be corroded.

34
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:05,200
 Materials which tend to form a nice thin, strong oxide layer that seals them off from further

35
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:08,160
 corrosion are said to have the property of passivity.

36
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:11,520
 And this oxide layer passivates them from further corrosion.

37
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:15,840
 Aluminium is a well-known example of this. If it suffers a scratch or an indentation or something,

38
00:02:15,840 --> 00:02:20,480
 it grows back a new thin film of oxide layer that protects it from further corrosion.

39
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:24,880
 Generally speaking, prevention of corrosion is achieved by sealing the anode from potential

40
00:02:24,880 --> 00:02:29,920
 cathodes by means of some sort of insulation. Passivity is only one type of insulation,

41
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:34,240
 and for materials which don't have this property, there are ways that you can prevent them from

42
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:38,640
 corroding. One very common approach is to use a surface coating like a paint or some sort of

43
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:42,960
 plating process. You need to be a little bit careful in the application and maintenance of these

44
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:47,360
 coatings, however, because if there is an imperfection, if there's a scratch, for example,

45
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:52,720
 corrosion can begin to grow inside under the coating. In some cases, corrosion can be presented

46
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:58,240
 by offering up a sacrifice. Here, you coat or plate the material that you want to protect in

47
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:03,040
 another material which is more susceptible to corrosion. This is the concept behind galvanization,

48
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:07,840
 a process you often see on mass manufactured components that need to live out in the elements.

49
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:11,440
 In this case, a zinc layer is placed on the outside of your steel components,

50
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:14,800
 and that zinc layer is more susceptible to corrosion than the steel is,

51
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:19,040
 meaning that the zinc needs to be consumed before the steel can. This usually takes quite a

52
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:24,720
 long time, and a combination of galvanization and surface coating and painting can be quite an

53
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:29,040
 effective way of protecting steel and iron components from rust. Materials which exhibit

54
00:03:29,040 --> 00:03:33,200
 passivity aren't immune from corrosion, and in particularly corrosive environments can still

55
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:38,240
 corrode. That being said, subjecting these materials to a process known as anodizing can actually

56
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:43,840
 encourage the growth of a thicker, stronger oxide layer, essentially by preloading the corrosion

57
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:48,480
 in a particular way, you can build a nice defense to future corrosion in the field.

58
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:52,800
 The susceptibility of a material to corrosion is largely dependent on its chemical makeup,

59
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:57,520
 the number of electrons that it has floating around in its upper shells. For corrosion between

60
00:03:57,520 --> 00:04:02,240
 two materials, however, a type called galvanic corrosion. A galvanic series chart like this one over

61
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:06,720
 here can give you an indication of the likely direction of attack, which material will act as the

62
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:11,920
 anode and be oxidized, and which material will act as the cathode, being reduced and suffering very

63
00:04:12,080 --> 00:04:16,880
 little to no damage. Corrosion of all types is accelerated in the presence of substances or conditions

64
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:22,320
 that are able to aid the underlying redox reaction. Very high temperatures can aid corrosion,

65
00:04:22,320 --> 00:04:27,760
 as can acids, and probably a very common example, salts. Not so much in Australia, but in many

66
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:32,560
 other countries where the conditions are generally a little bit cooler, road salt is used to melt

67
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:37,280
 snow on roads. As cars drive around on this salty snow, they tend to kick it up onto their

68
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:42,560
 underside, and as the snow melts, you've now got this perfect slurry of corrosion forming liquid

69
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:47,200
 on the underside of your car. This is why in countries like Japan or Europe, rust seems to be much

70
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:51,520
 more common on cars than it is here in Australia where, to be honest, conditions are usually pretty

71
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:56,240
 mild. That'll probably do us for corrosion. If you want to take away some key points from this video,

72
00:04:56,240 --> 00:05:00,960
 corrosion is the result of an electrochemical process known as a redox reaction. In this kind of

73
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:06,080
 reaction, one material is oxidized, giving up electrons, and one material is reduced, taking in

74
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:11,040
 electrons. The oxidized material corrodes, and the result of this corrosion is usually a

75
00:05:11,040 --> 00:05:15,120
 worsening of material properties. This isn't true of all materials, with some forming a

76
00:05:15,120 --> 00:05:19,520
 passivating layer that protects them from further corrosion and can actually have better material

77
00:05:19,520 --> 00:05:24,080
 properties. Corrosion can be prevented by sealing materials off from potential cathodes using a

78
00:05:24,080 --> 00:05:29,120
 paint or a surface coating, and corrosion can be worsened in the presence of salts and acids and

79
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:33,600
 temperature and other things that have the ability to worsen the underlying redox reaction.

80
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:36,960
 There's a little bit more info about corrosion on the textbook page for this video,

81
00:05:36,960 --> 00:05:46,240
 I encourage you to check it out.
